<section class="how">
    <h2>How to Use This Calculator</h2>
    <p>Choose your unit system first. Most US drivers use imperial, which means miles and gallons. Drivers in Canada, Europe, and Australia typically work with kilometers and liters.</p>
    <p>Enter the distance you drove and how much fuel you filled up to cover that distance. The most accurate way to measure this is to fill your tank completely, drive your usual route, then fill up again and note the exact gallons or liters it takes to top off the tank. That refill amount is your actual fuel consumption for that trip.</p>
    <p>Add your current local fuel price and your estimated yearly mileage to see your annual fuel cost. This number is useful for comparing the real running cost of different vehicles before you buy.</p>
</section>

<section class="formula">
    <h2>How Gas Mileage Is Calculated</h2>
    <p>The formula is different depending on which system you use.</p>
    <p><strong>MPG (miles per gallon) = Miles driven / Gallons used</strong></p>
    <p><strong>L/100km (liters per 100 kilometers) = (Liters used / km driven) x 100</strong></p>
    <p>Note that higher MPG means better efficiency. But with L/100km it is the opposite: a lower number is better. A vehicle at 6 L/100km is more efficient than one at 10 L/100km. This trips people up when comparing across units.</p>
    <p>To convert MPG to L/100km, divide 235.21 by your MPG figure. So 30 MPG equals 235.21 / 30, which is approximately 7.84 L/100km.</p>
    <p><strong>Cost per mile = Fuel price per gallon / MPG</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Annual fuel cost = Cost per mile x Annual miles driven</strong></p>
</section>

