<section class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>What is considered good gas mileage?</h3>
    <p>For a passenger car, anything above 30 MPG is considered good. Compact cars and sedans often hit 35 to 42 MPG. Hybrids typically reach 45 to 55 MPG combined. Full-size trucks and SUVs usually sit between 16 and 24 MPG. Electric vehicles do not have a direct MPG equivalent but are often rated as MPGe, or miles per gallon equivalent.</p>

    <h3>Why does my real-world MPG differ from the EPA estimate?</h3>
    <p>EPA estimates are measured under lab conditions designed to be repeatable. Real-world driving involves traffic, hills, climate control use, cold engine starts, and varying speeds. Most drivers see fuel economy 10% to 20% below the EPA label in everyday driving. Highway driving tends to be closer to the estimate than city driving.</p>

    <h3>How can I improve my gas mileage?</h3>
    <p>The biggest gains come from steady driving speeds. Aggressive acceleration and heavy braking can reduce fuel economy by 15% to 30% at highway speeds. Checking tire pressure is also worthwhile since under-inflated tires increase rolling resistance. Removing heavy items from your trunk and using cruise control on the highway help too.</p>

    <h3>How do gas prices affect annual fuel costs?</h3>
    <p>The relationship is completely linear. If gas prices rise by 20%, your annual fuel bill rises by exactly 20%, assuming the same driving distance and vehicle. The only factor you can control is how much you drive or what vehicle you drive. A more fuel-efficient car insulates you from price spikes more effectively than a less efficient one.</p>
</section>

